  "content": {
    "id": "p3-q14-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "14",
    "prompt": "Determine the value of $k$ such that $y=kx$ bisects the acute angle between the lines $y=x$ and $y=3x$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$k=\\dfrac{1+\\sqrt5}{2}$.\n\nUse the tangent difference formula to equate the two angles.",
      "worked": "Let $\\theta$ be the angle between $y=kx$ and each of the given lines. For the acute-angle bisector, $1<k<3$. Using the tangent difference formula,\n$$\\begin{align*}\\tan\\theta&=\\frac{k-1}{1+k},\\\\\\tan\\theta&=\\frac{3-k}{1+3k}.\\end{align*}$$\nEquating these expressions,\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{k-1}{1+k}&=\\frac{3-k}{1+3k},\\\\(k-1)(1+3k)&=(3-k)(1+k),\\\\3k^2-2k-1&=3+2k-k^2,\\\\4k^2-4k-4&=0,\\\\k^2-k-1&=0,\\\\k&=\\frac{1\\pm\\sqrt5}{2}.\\end{align*}$$\nSince $1<k<3$, $k=\\dfrac{1+\\sqrt5}{2}$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 85
  },
